WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.434
-Welcome back to our show.
Thank you for doing talk with us

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:05.968
and thank you for doing
that song with us earlier.

00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:07.801
I appreciate that.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:09.300
You singing "Margaritaville"

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.200
was something I don't think
everyone thought we would see.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.167
I want to talk to you
about going on tour.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:17.367
You're about to start off --
kick off another tour.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:18.801
Are you excited?
Are you preparing?

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:20.200
What are you doing?

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.367
-Um, yeah, I'm so --

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:27.167
I'm so excited to play again,
because there was just --

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:29.367
Yeah, there was a period of time
where I thought -- I was like,

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.968
"God, Do I need to, like,
find another job?"

00:00:31.968 --> 00:00:34.667
-I mean, I guess
during the pandemic also --

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.434
-Yeah, all kind of singing
and performing was gone,

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:39.901
and that's kind of all
I've really known.

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:42.667
But what's really funny about
it, we've done some shows --

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.868
Because the last two years
feel like I kind of blinked.

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:46.801
Our last show was in 2019,

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:49.200
and so when I came back
to play again, I was like,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.267
"Wait. Was I just here?
Did this just happen?"

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:54.601
But it's been -- Do you know
what's the nicest thing,

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:56.868
is going and seeing everyone
who come to the shows

00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:59.801
because they make such an effort
and everybody dresses up.

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:01.000
The Florence + The Machine show

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.634
is, like, a fully immersive
experience.

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:06.300
-Yeah, I've seen people
dress like you.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.100
Like, identically.
And they actually --

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.367
Yeah, I've seen it.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.167
-But it's nice for me
because I play a show

00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:14.234
and I also get to watch a show

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:15.667
because, like,
the whole front row

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:19.801
will just be
in these amazing outfits.

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:20.834
What did it --
I think "Rolling Stone"

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:22.601
described it as, like,

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:25.234
"Euphoria" meets
the renaissance fair.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:26.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:29.968
Which is a really good,
accurate description, I thought.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.634
-Yeah, I would take that.
But, I mean, look at this.

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:32.767
By the way,
look at this picture.

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:34.868
This is how cool you are.
Come on.

00:01:34.868 --> 00:01:37.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:37.501 --> 00:01:40.300
You know what I mean?
That's your move.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.868
I love that. Gorgeous.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:46.033
And I was like, "Okay.
I love that. That's your move."

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.834
I wonder how long
you've been doing that.

00:01:48.834 --> 00:01:52.133
And I got actually --
I found a baby photo of you.

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.367
And here's Baby Florence.
With your mom, Evelyn.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.968
Look at the-- Look at the --
the flower crown.

00:01:57.968 --> 00:01:59.868
-There's already a flower crown.

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:01.133
-You were always
into flower crowns.

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:03.033
-I know.
Always in a flower crown.

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:06.067
Born in one, will be buried
in one, I think.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:08.834
-Will be buried in one.
You were saying, too --

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:14.767
I go, "Where did your
kind of thing come from?"

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.968
Where did your
whole identity come from

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:19.133
with Florence + The Machine?

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:22.133
-So I think -- I don't know.

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.534
I was thinking a lot about this,

00:02:23.534 --> 00:02:25.501
and I sometimes wonder
if the whole thing

00:02:25.501 --> 00:02:27.200
of Florence + The Machine
is, like,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.801
an amalgamation of things
that I loved when I was a kid.

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:32.133
So I feel like Florence
+ The Machine is a mix of,

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:36.400
like, Rogue from X-Men
with a Victorian ghost

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.434
and then, like, mermaids,
and I wonder if one day

00:02:40.434 --> 00:02:43.100
I'll wake up
and I'm back in my childhood bed

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.934
and it was a dream.

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:46.434
I just kind of, like,
made it up.

00:02:46.434 --> 00:02:48.667
-Oh, wow. And you made up -- And
I love that I'm in your dream.

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.133
This is awesome.
-Yeah, I know!

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:52.100
-This is the best thing
I've heard all week.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.601
I looked it up because I was
like, "Oh, Rogue from X-Men."

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:56.334
I just wanted to look it up
and see what it is,

00:02:56.334 --> 00:02:58.067
and I kind of get it.

00:02:58.067 --> 00:02:59.400
Yeah, I see this.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.534
-It was redheads, so it was,
like, Rogue from X-Men,

00:03:01.534 --> 00:03:04.634
the Little Mermaid,
Anne of Green Gables.

00:03:04.634 --> 00:03:06.634
Pippi Longstocking
was huge for me.

00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:08.501
-Oh, yeah.
-So, yeah.

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:10.234
-Like, what were early gigs
like for you?

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:11.834
Do you remember
where you started out?

00:03:11.834 --> 00:03:13.901
What was one of your first gigs?

00:03:13.901 --> 00:03:18.234
-Um, okay.
So a lot of my first "gigs" --

00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:19.934
-Yeah, sorry.

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:22.667
-Gigs were -- I just kind of
pretended I had a band,

00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:24.234
and I didn't really have a band.

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:26.167
But there was -- Yeah.

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:28.634
So there's a lot of imagination
that goes on.

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:31.100
There's a lot of pretending,
as you can see.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.434
So Florence + The Machine
kind of started out

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:37.167
with everyone in the Southland
and indie scene all had bands,

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:39.200
and so I would just show up
to these club nights

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.501
and be like, "Yeah, I have
a band. I have a band, too."

00:03:41.501 --> 00:03:43.968
And that's how I got booked
for my first show,

00:03:43.968 --> 00:03:46.701
by pretending I had a band
that I didn't have.

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:47.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:49.767
I had no band members.

00:03:49.767 --> 00:03:52.033
I had a name --
I had the name of the band

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:53.501
and saying I had a band,

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:55.834
but there were no members
of this band.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:57.234
I had to find some members.

00:03:57.234 --> 00:03:59.033
-But what would happen when
you get booked? You're like,

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:00.968
"Hey, I just got booked.
I need someone to play with."

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:02.167
-That was exactly
what happened.

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:03.667
Because I got booked
for my first show

00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:07.000
by auditioning
in a night-club toilet

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.434
and saying I had a band
and just singing.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.801
-Wow.
-And then I said --

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:14.267
I need to find a guitar player
because I have a show in a week.

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:17.000
And I was, like, on a bill,
yeah, so --

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.968
-Isn't it funny, the stories --

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:21.868
No one knows all the stories and
the hard work and the hustling.

00:04:21.868 --> 00:04:23.934
You had to audition in a toilet
to get to where you are.

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:26.033
-No, I did, yeah, but bathrooms
have very good acoustics.

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:28.200
-They do actually.
Yeah, you are right.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.367
-They really do.

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:32.133
"Dance Fever"
comes out this Friday.

00:04:32.133 --> 00:04:33.767
It's beautiful. It's fun.

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:36.501
It's -- There's bangers on here.

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:39.601
Like, I think my jam
right now is -- I like "Free"

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:41.033
right now,
is what I'm jamming out to.

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:42.701
But I love it so much.

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:45.067
You've been working on this
for a few years.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:47.734
-I started working on it
in 2019.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:49.567
As soon as kind of
the last record --

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:51.334
that cycle ended, I --

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:53.868
The album is kind of
about that fever of creativity.

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:55.801
So as soon as I finished
playing the last show,

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:59.200
it was, like,
all these songs arrived in 2019,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.767
and I set off to New York

00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:05.534
in January --

00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:07.300
end of February 2020,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.767
just thinking,
"This is gonna go so well."

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:12.200
-Wow!

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.934
Boy, were you wrong. Wow. Wow.

00:05:14.934 --> 00:05:16.701
-I was like,
"This is gonna go so well."

00:05:16.701 --> 00:05:19.634
I had three months booked
at Electric Lady.

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:21.601
-Oh, yeah.
-I've been --

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:23.200
I had so many songs
written already.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.534
I was like, "Do you know what?
I think we can just

00:05:24.534 --> 00:05:26.133
get this done fast.

00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:29.601
I really think this is
gonna be easy, this one."

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:31.033
-And then this happened.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:34.267
-And then my mum called,
and my mum never calls me

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.667
because she's very busy.

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:38.167
She only calls me
if something --

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:40.467
I don't know if anyone else
has -- But she only calls --

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:42.100
I looked down,
I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.400
Something terrible must have
happened or somebody died."

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.234
Like, why is she --
-The only reason why mom calls.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:50.934
-I was like, "Oh, my God.
It's Professor Evelyn Welch."

00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:55.067
I was thinking about this story
before I came about the album.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.601
I was like,
"I need to look at my phone."

00:05:56.601 --> 00:05:59.601
Because she can't actually
be "Professor Evelyn Welch"

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:02.033
in my phone.
Like, she should be "Mum."

00:06:02.033 --> 00:06:04.667
-Mum, yeah.
Did she change the contact?

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:05.968
-She's not.

00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:08.033
In my phone,
I've full-named her.

00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:11.067
It comes up as Evelyn Welch.
And I don't -- I'm like --

00:06:11.067 --> 00:06:13.267
That's the most British thing
I've ever done.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:15.701
-[ Laughter ]
-That is very British of you.

00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:17.767
-Why don't I have my mum
in my phone?

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:19.701
-Your own mom, yeah. Or mum.

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:22.234
-"Oh, Evelyn Welch is calling.
Time to go."

00:06:22.234 --> 00:06:24.534
-And she was like, "Hey.
This is serious. Get home."

00:06:24.534 --> 00:06:26.033
-I think maybe I full-named her

00:06:26.033 --> 00:06:28.200
because she's quite
a serious person.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.968
And she was like, "Yeah,
you should come home now."

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:35.801
But we also thought, "Well,
I'll be back in a month.

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:37.434
See you then."

00:06:37.434 --> 00:06:39.167
No, I didn't actually
get back to New York

00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:42.367
to work with Jack
for a year and a half.

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:43.901
-I think we all thought that.

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:45.467
This was going to be finished
in a couple of weeks.

00:06:45.467 --> 00:06:48.834
And cut to two
and a half years later, yeah.

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:51.968
It turned out unbelievable.
And we need entertainment.

00:06:51.968 --> 00:06:54.400
We need good music.
We need solid stuff.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.033
This is -- And you said Jack.
Jack Antonoff.

00:06:57.033 --> 00:07:00.400
-Yeah, Jack Antonoff and myself
and Dave Bailey produced it.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.000
-Ah, talented people
right there.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.934
You're doing -- What song
are you doing tonight for us?

00:07:04.934 --> 00:07:06.367
-"My Love."
-Yeah.

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:08.968
And do you want to set that up?
What's "My Love" about?

00:07:08.968 --> 00:07:12.334
-It started off
as just a really sad poem,

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:14.400
but when I tried
to put it acoustically,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.767
it was too sad.

00:07:16.767 --> 00:07:19.100
It was just like,
"This is too bleak."

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:20.634
-Really?
-Yeah, I couldn't --

00:07:20.634 --> 00:07:22.200
It was just sort of --

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:26.200
I needed something to kind of
turn the sadness on its head

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.467
and add some euphoria.

00:07:27.467 --> 00:07:29.467
I needed catharsis at that time.

00:07:29.467 --> 00:07:33.000
And so, yeah,
me and Dave Bailey,

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.234
who was also at that point
stuck at home

00:07:35.234 --> 00:07:37.067
having the biggest hit
of his career

00:07:37.067 --> 00:07:39.501
and doing all of his interviews
from his kitchen.

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:43.267
So we got together
and kind of made this song.

00:07:43.267 --> 00:07:44.901
-And it's a jam,
so get ready, guys.

00:07:44.901 --> 00:07:46.501
It's unbelievable.

00:07:46.501 --> 00:07:49.067
"Dance Fever"
is out this Friday.

00:07:49.067 --> 00:07:51.667
Florence will be performing
later in the show.

00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:53.300
Florence Welch, everybody.

